    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d863">

                <pb facs="0935" n="649" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg649"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 341.622a/151</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-06-12T16:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 12, 1916, 4 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 5.30 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4446. Your 3402, June 8, 4 p. m. Lord Robert Cecil, speaking for Sir Edward Grey,
                    informs me that the passengers taken from the <hi rendition="#italic">China</hi>
                    are in Australia and that immediately on my presentation of the matter on June
                    9, he asked the Admiralty what arrangements had been made for their release and
                    transportation. He seemed surprised, as I was, that they had not already been
                    released. To-day he expresses the hope of a very early answer from the
                    Admiralty.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
